WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.251 --> 00:00:03.253
“I built a giant death trap.”

00:00:03.586 --> 00:00:05.797
On October 7th, 2023

00:00:05.797 --> 00:00:09.009
Mr. Beast posted this video 
with the contestant

00:00:09.009 --> 00:00:11.177
traveling through a series of obstacles.

00:00:11.511 --> 00:00:13.805
It was a video sponsored by a mobile game

00:00:13.805 --> 00:00:15.348
called Stumble Guys.

00:00:15.849 --> 00:00:16.891
Along with CGI

00:00:16.891 --> 00:00:18.935
the pivotal moments in the trap...

00:00:19.019 --> 00:00:20.770
included obvious cuts.

00:00:23.982 --> 00:00:27.569
The second trap ends with contestant Zack 
diving under a door.

00:00:27.944 --> 00:00:30.280
See how his leg is here at this point.

00:00:30.822 --> 00:00:32.657
When it switches to an alternate angle...

00:00:32.991 --> 00:00:34.200
his leg is here.

00:00:35.285 --> 00:00:37.203
See the shoes and no bag.

00:00:37.829 --> 00:00:39.998
Now here's a bag and no shoes.

00:00:41.082 --> 00:00:44.836
The video has more than 150 million views.

00:00:45.253 --> 00:00:47.047
[overlapping Mr. Beast intros]

00:00:48.006 --> 00:00:52.135
Around half of Mr. Beast's videos 
are now game shows.

00:00:52.135 --> 00:00:54.763
“$111,500.”

00:00:54.888 --> 00:00:57.182
Their questionable reality 
is part of a history

00:00:57.182 --> 00:00:59.100
that goes back to a national scandal...

00:00:59.392 --> 00:01:00.518
in the 1950s.

00:01:01.019 --> 00:01:02.854
It includes congressional hearings...

00:01:02.937 --> 00:01:06.399
“The dramatic climax of the probe 
of fixed and rigged quiz shows.”

00:01:06.483 --> 00:01:07.525
...castaways in Borneo...

00:01:08.068 --> 00:01:09.277
“And the tribe has spoken.”

00:01:09.402 --> 00:01:12.405
...and a brief era when cheating 
on a game show...

00:01:12.572 --> 00:01:13.948
was a federal crime.

00:01:13.948 --> 00:01:15.784
“...wins $100,000...”

00:01:17.494 --> 00:01:21.664
In the 1940s, television had begun 
advances across the nation.

00:01:23.374 --> 00:01:27.504
And by 1955, a game show called
“The $64,000 Question”

00:01:27.504 --> 00:01:28.838
debuted on CBS.

00:01:29.005 --> 00:01:32.467
“Yes, the $64,000 question.”

00:01:32.967 --> 00:01:35.053
A contestant in an isolation booth

00:01:35.053 --> 00:01:37.597
was asked increasingly difficult questions.

00:01:37.972 --> 00:01:40.809
The design implied security and integrity.

00:01:41.142 --> 00:01:44.145
Questions were delivered straight from 
a bank vault to the set.

00:01:44.354 --> 00:01:45.647
It was sponsored by Revlon...

00:01:45.939 --> 00:01:48.650
and was a fabulously successful TV show...

00:01:48.858 --> 00:01:51.736
topping ratings with 16 million viewers.

00:01:51.736 --> 00:01:55.406
Almost half of all houses that owned TVs 
were watching it.

00:01:56.783 --> 00:01:58.451
It started a quiz show boom. 

00:01:59.285 --> 00:02:00.954
These shows included Dotto...

00:02:01.287 --> 00:02:04.290
a game where contestants 
answered trivia questions

00:02:04.290 --> 00:02:06.334
to unlock a connect-the-dots puzzle.

00:02:06.835 --> 00:02:07.919
The less said the better...

00:02:08.211 --> 00:02:09.087
And Twenty One...

00:02:09.587 --> 00:02:11.256
“Welcome back to Twenty One.”

00:02:11.256 --> 00:02:14.592
...a show where two contestants 
appeared in isolation booths.

00:02:14.968 --> 00:02:19.055
Each answered questions for points, 
hoping to reach 21.

00:02:19.264 --> 00:02:23.143
It was sponsored by Geritol, a supplement
that claimed to help with...

00:02:23.143 --> 00:02:23.977
“Tired blood.”

00:02:24.519 --> 00:02:25.728
“Tired blood.”

00:02:25.770 --> 00:02:29.232
The show and trend peaked in 1957...

00:02:29.524 --> 00:02:32.735
when Time magazine put 
long-running quiz champ

00:02:32.735 --> 00:02:34.445
Charles Van Doren on the cover.

00:02:36.281 --> 00:02:38.199
His streak began with the defeat of...

00:02:38.199 --> 00:02:41.327
“Herbert Stempel, our 29 year old 
GI College student.”

00:02:41.411 --> 00:02:43.204
“You win $20,000!”

00:02:43.246 --> 00:02:44.455
“Congratulations!”

00:02:45.456 --> 00:02:48.084
Van Doren became a family friendly celebrity.

00:02:48.209 --> 00:02:51.880
TV's own health restoring antidote 
to Elvis Presley

00:02:51.880 --> 00:02:54.549
as part of the literary Van Doren clan.

00:02:54.549 --> 00:02:57.886
A family well known in New York 
intellectual circles.

00:02:58.094 --> 00:03:01.639
After his $129,000 streak ended

00:03:01.639 --> 00:03:03.975
an amount worth 1.3 million today....

00:03:04.184 --> 00:03:07.478
Van Doren became the Today Show's 
cultural correspondent.

00:03:08.229 --> 00:03:09.522
Just two months later

00:03:09.522 --> 00:03:11.107
the shows were so popular...

00:03:11.149 --> 00:03:14.360
that Time magazine was asking: 
Are quiz shows rigged?

00:03:15.069 --> 00:03:17.614
“There's all this rigamarole about locked vaults.”

00:03:17.655 --> 00:03:18.865
One insider said.

00:03:19.032 --> 00:03:21.451
“But who has the key to the locked vault?”

00:03:21.492 --> 00:03:23.161
“The producer, of course.”

00:03:23.244 --> 00:03:26.039
Soon everyone would be asking the same question.

00:03:26.122 --> 00:03:28.625
In summer of ‘58, 
Dotto went blotto.

00:03:29.125 --> 00:03:31.502
The show was canceled after a contestant

00:03:31.502 --> 00:03:34.505
found an answer filled notebook backstage.

00:03:34.714 --> 00:03:37.634
A slew of shows were implicated 
in “quiz guilt”

00:03:37.634 --> 00:03:40.386
over the next few years, 
including Twenty One.

00:03:40.386 --> 00:03:41.262
Herbert Stempel...

00:03:41.471 --> 00:03:43.973
the Twenty One contestant 
who had lost to Van Doren...

00:03:44.307 --> 00:03:46.726
made claims that Twenty One was rigged

00:03:46.726 --> 00:03:50.855
with producers providing him questions 
and thorough stage direction.

00:03:50.897 --> 00:03:52.690
“Your time is up, for example, 
for five points

00:03:52.690 --> 00:03:53.983
which would give you a 21.”

00:03:54.234 --> 00:03:57.528
“What motion picture won 
the Academy Award for 1955?”

00:04:01.366 --> 00:04:02.951
I don't remember anything about that.

00:04:04.327 --> 00:04:07.121
The producers claimed Stempel 
was blackmailing them.

00:04:07.372 --> 00:04:10.124
The US House Subcommittee on 
Legislative Oversight...

00:04:10.458 --> 00:04:12.669
began hearings on the quiz shows.

00:04:14.921 --> 00:04:15.964
The report began:

00:04:15.964 --> 00:04:18.549
“It is one thing to arouse and hold the attention

00:04:18.549 --> 00:04:21.219
of the viewing public by programs which are openly...

00:04:21.552 --> 00:04:23.096
and avowedly pure fiction.”

00:04:25.515 --> 00:04:28.142
“And should be known as such 
to any reasonable person.”

00:04:28.184 --> 00:04:29.394
“It is quite another thing....

00:04:29.602 --> 00:04:32.981
however, when the airwaves belonging to the people...

00:04:33.356 --> 00:04:34.857
whose free use has been licensed

00:04:34.857 --> 00:04:38.653
by the Federal Communications Commission 
are used deceitfully...

00:04:38.903 --> 00:04:40.655
to exploit for private profit

00:04:40.655 --> 00:04:42.532
the interests of the viewing public.”

00:04:42.865 --> 00:04:45.159
Stempel unloaded details of the coaching...

00:04:45.368 --> 00:04:47.745
in addition to questions and answers.

00:04:47.954 --> 00:04:51.040
Like that hey refused to turn on 
the air conditioning in his booth...

00:04:51.457 --> 00:04:54.252
“Thereby causing me to perspire profusely.”

00:04:54.544 --> 00:04:57.839
“Van Doren arrives to apologize 
and attempt to explain

00:04:57.839 --> 00:05:00.258
to the millions whose friendship 
and respect he had won.”

00:05:00.425 --> 00:05:03.428
Van Doren eventually admitted in testimony...

00:05:03.970 --> 00:05:06.306
“He instructed me how to answer the questions.”

00:05:06.597 --> 00:05:08.766
“To pause before certain of the answers.”

00:05:09.559 --> 00:05:11.978
[mumbling lines]

00:05:12.395 --> 00:05:15.523
“On this first occasion and on several subsequent ones...

00:05:15.898 --> 00:05:18.276
he gave me a script to memorize.”

00:05:18.568 --> 00:05:20.862
“And before the program he took back the script...

00:05:21.154 --> 00:05:22.822
and rehearsed me in my part.”

00:05:23.114 --> 00:05:25.450
“But his statement is a rueful and moving

00:05:25.450 --> 00:05:28.119
realization that for his wealth and fame...

00:05:28.369 --> 00:05:30.330
he paid a bitterly high price.”

00:05:30.496 --> 00:05:33.082
The investigations resulted 
in Van Doren

00:05:33.082 --> 00:05:36.002
losing his job as a Today Show 
correspondent...

00:05:36.294 --> 00:05:38.588
and in the cancellation of other quiz shows.

00:05:39.130 --> 00:05:43.176
Though he and others pled guilty of perjury
in earlier testimony...

00:05:43.426 --> 00:05:44.010
he was freed.

00:05:44.886 --> 00:05:48.639
But more importantly, the Communications 
Act of 1934 was amended...

00:05:48.848 --> 00:05:51.309
to include prohibited practices

00:05:51.309 --> 00:05:53.978
in case of contests of intellectual knowledge...

00:05:54.312 --> 00:05:56.230
intellectual skill or chance...

00:05:56.606 --> 00:06:01.486
making it unlawful with intent to deceive 
the listening or viewing public...

00:06:01.778 --> 00:06:04.155
with up to a year of imprisonment.

00:06:04.322 --> 00:06:06.032
The FCC was in charge...

00:06:06.282 --> 00:06:08.701
and rigging the game show 
was a federal crime.

00:06:09.660 --> 00:06:10.828
So what happened?

00:06:10.828 --> 00:06:13.039
Well, I was actually a huge Survivor fan

00:06:13.039 --> 00:06:15.792
and I found the whole Stacey Stillman

00:06:15.792 --> 00:06:18.419
Mark Burnett controversy 
to be super interesting.

00:06:18.461 --> 00:06:22.757
After the 1950s, a combination of 
falling ratings and public distrust...

00:06:23.049 --> 00:06:25.134
kept game shows lower profile.

00:06:25.426 --> 00:06:26.594
Until a renaissance.

00:06:26.844 --> 00:06:31.724
“The first person who refuses to eat a bug 
loses immunity for their tribe.”

00:06:32.517 --> 00:06:35.937
The long running show Survivor 
made its debut in 2000.

00:06:36.020 --> 00:06:39.190
Contestants competed in challenges, but crucially...

00:06:39.399 --> 00:06:42.735
elimination occurred via vote 
at Tribal Council.

00:06:42.985 --> 00:06:43.903
In 2001...

00:06:44.028 --> 00:06:47.740
an original Survivor cast member 
claimed the show was rigged.

00:06:47.782 --> 00:06:50.743
Essentially, Stacy alleged that 
in the first season

00:06:50.743 --> 00:06:53.830
in order to save a more
ratings-friendly contestant...

00:06:54.038 --> 00:06:56.207
“He's fat, but he's good.”

00:06:56.249 --> 00:06:58.626
Mark Burnett, the executive producer 
on the series

00:06:58.626 --> 00:07:02.004
approached two other contestants 
on the beach and essentially said...

00:07:02.046 --> 00:07:05.383
to vote for Stacey instead of 
that other contestant

00:07:05.383 --> 00:07:07.552
that they were planning on voting for.

00:07:07.718 --> 00:07:10.847
Years later, Attorney George Brietigam 
started to wonder...

00:07:11.055 --> 00:07:14.225
did the old quiz show laws 
apply in that case?

00:07:14.267 --> 00:07:19.522
I filed a Freedom of Information Act
request from 2000 to 2017.

00:07:19.814 --> 00:07:22.400
But the lawsuit entered into a private settlement

00:07:22.400 --> 00:07:25.153
before any FCC investigation commenced.

00:07:25.445 --> 00:07:29.907
This followed a pattern in the 2000s
of a weakened quiz show law.

00:07:30.908 --> 00:07:32.910
The truth is, they're actually 
not really fighting

00:07:32.910 --> 00:07:35.455
these reality shows using that statute.

00:07:35.663 --> 00:07:37.415
The closest thing that I found was

00:07:37.415 --> 00:07:41.294
there was a planned Fox gameshow 
called Our Little Genius.

00:07:41.544 --> 00:07:44.547
“He also happens to be one of the 
smartest people in America.”

00:07:45.339 --> 00:07:47.925
The planned 2009 show cast kids...

00:07:47.967 --> 00:07:50.344
as quiz competitors to adults.

00:07:50.595 --> 00:07:53.264
One complainant to the FCC said their child

00:07:53.264 --> 00:07:55.683
had been registered as a calculus expert...

00:07:55.808 --> 00:07:58.311
but was suddenly switched to music theory.

00:07:58.394 --> 00:08:00.897
It says a producer passed them note names...

00:08:01.189 --> 00:08:03.065
like the hemidemisemiquaver...

00:08:03.274 --> 00:08:05.485
as the British name for the 64th note.

00:08:05.651 --> 00:08:07.570
The complainant ended saying...

00:08:07.904 --> 00:08:10.656
“I urge you to conduct in investigation.”

00:08:10.656 --> 00:08:12.408
That investigation was ultimately

00:08:12.408 --> 00:08:15.203
abandoned by the FCC because 
what the production did was

00:08:15.203 --> 00:08:17.955
it essentially said, “Hey, we're not going 
to broadcast this.”

00:08:18.247 --> 00:08:19.332
We don't broadcast this

00:08:19.332 --> 00:08:20.791
you guys don't even have jurisdiction.

00:08:20.791 --> 00:08:25.463
And it's true: The FCC only has 
jurisdiction for broadcast.

00:08:25.671 --> 00:08:26.339
Cable?

00:08:26.589 --> 00:08:27.798
Do whatever you want.

00:08:28.007 --> 00:08:28.841
Internet?

00:08:28.841 --> 00:08:30.051
Nobody cares.

00:08:30.176 --> 00:08:33.346
You can shoot something for broadcast

00:08:33.346 --> 00:08:35.890
but as long as it doesn't actually air, 
you're not in violation.

00:08:38.476 --> 00:08:41.395
And the time line of game show 
investigations overlaps

00:08:41.395 --> 00:08:44.106
with a change in consumption options.

00:08:44.315 --> 00:08:47.109
Game shows being real was possibly...

00:08:47.568 --> 00:08:48.861
just a window in time.

00:08:49.612 --> 00:08:52.907
[slowly]
“Three... two... one.”

00:08:53.449 --> 00:08:54.575
But does it matter though?

00:08:55.201 --> 00:08:57.286
“That was so—I’m speechless!”

00:08:57.328 --> 00:09:00.289
Everyone assumes that because 
I wrote this paper

00:09:00.289 --> 00:09:03.626
I am in support of stronger regulations

00:09:03.626 --> 00:09:05.169
stronger FCC enforcement...

00:09:05.878 --> 00:09:07.213
I really am not.

00:09:07.964 --> 00:09:09.423
I think we're at the point

00:09:09.423 --> 00:09:12.260
everyone knows that what's on TV 
is often fake.

00:09:12.552 --> 00:09:15.846
Everyone knows what's on the internet 
is usually not real.

00:09:23.729 --> 00:09:26.691
Mr. Beast has just one FCC license...

00:09:26.941 --> 00:09:30.236
to help coordinate radio communication 
for his productions.

00:09:30.987 --> 00:09:34.824
The Mr. Beast team did not respond 
to our multiple requests for comment.

00:09:35.449 --> 00:09:39.161
And in 1959, Geritol and Sominex sales soared...

00:09:39.579 --> 00:09:41.330
while Van Doren sweated.

00:09:41.872 --> 00:09:45.251
Stumble Guys is currently 
number six in action

00:09:45.251 --> 00:09:46.210
on the App Store.

00:09:46.544 --> 00:09:48.254
“I want to tell you about Stumble Guys.”

00:09:49.839 --> 00:09:50.715
Hey, everybody.

00:09:50.715 --> 00:09:51.757
My name's Phil Edwards.

00:09:51.757 --> 00:09:53.593
I'm the producer of this episode.

00:09:53.593 --> 00:09:55.928
If you're anything like me, you might be 
a little worried now

00:09:55.928 --> 00:09:57.263
that you have tired blood.

00:09:57.513 --> 00:09:59.849
“Tired blood, tired blood.”

00:09:59.890 --> 00:10:01.058
If you're a fan of Vox

00:10:01.058 --> 00:10:02.685
whether you've been watching us for years

00:10:02.685 --> 00:10:04.186
or this is your first video

00:10:04.186 --> 00:10:06.939
I think that our mission 
is still pretty clear...

00:10:07.148 --> 00:10:09.775
that we want to give you 
high-quality journalism

00:10:09.775 --> 00:10:11.068
and we want to keep it free.

00:10:11.068 --> 00:10:12.612
We don't want to put it behind a paywall.

00:10:12.820 --> 00:10:13.904
Now, most newsrooms

00:10:13.904 --> 00:10:16.866
they’ll make money through either 
advertising or subscriptions

00:10:16.866 --> 00:10:19.118
but there are a couple big issues 
with those models...

00:10:19.410 --> 00:10:21.245
especially for a channel like ours.

00:10:21.621 --> 00:10:24.665
First, advertising goes up and down 
with the economy.

00:10:25.041 --> 00:10:26.584
We often only know a few months out

00:10:26.584 --> 00:10:29.712
what our revenue will be, which makes it 
pretty hard to plan.

00:10:29.795 --> 00:10:32.506
Second, we are not in the subscriptions business.

00:10:32.798 --> 00:10:34.925
Vox is here to help everyone understand

00:10:34.925 --> 00:10:38.012
complicated issues, not just the ones 
who can pay for stuff.

00:10:38.054 --> 00:10:40.431
We think it's important that 
everybody be able to get to it

00:10:40.431 --> 00:10:41.766
and we can't do it if we have a paywall.

00:10:41.891 --> 00:10:43.934
So if those values sound like your values...

00:10:44.185 --> 00:10:45.519
you can help us reach that goal.

00:10:45.728 --> 00:10:49.857
Help us get to 95,000 gifts 
by the end of the year...

00:10:49.940 --> 00:10:52.860
by going to www.vox.com/give-now.

